\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Different domains for the form, elimination and trace ratio}
\date{2026-10-18}

\p{On the [arithmetic family](fdip-000U), a nondegenerate
full form need not permit a chosen elimination, and an
invertible effective form need not give a regular trace ratio.
With #{s=t+2}, #{f=t+1} and
#{\Delta=3(2t+1)(2t+3)}, the exact regular loci in #{X_q} are
##{\begin{aligned}
\text{Schur matrix:}&\quad X_{\mathrm{elim}}=D_{X_q}(s),\\
\text{trace ratio:}&\quad U_R=D_{X_q}(f).
\end{aligned}}
Their common domain is #{X_{\mathrm{obs}}=D_{X_q}(sf)}.
These claims follow from the calculations below, extending the
[real Schur example](fdip-000F) and
[trace-ratio calculation](fdip-000I) over the declared rings.}

\proof{
\p{Order coordinates by retained #{(0,2)} and eliminated
#{(1,3)}. The diagonal blocks of #{Q} are
#{A=C_{\mathrm{block}}=sI_2}, and the off-diagonal block is
##{B=\begin{pmatrix}-1&-t\\-t&-1\end{pmatrix}.}
On #{R_{\mathrm{elim}}}, the Schur matrix is
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=A-BC_{\mathrm{block}}^{-1}B^T\\
&=\frac1s\begin{pmatrix}4t+3&-2t\\-2t&4t+3\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Indeed #{BB^T} has diagonal #{1+t^2} and off-diagonal #{2t},
and #{s^2-(1+t^2)=4t+3}. Moreover
##{\det S=\frac{(4t+3)^2-4t^2}{s^2}
=\frac{\Delta}{s^2},}
a unit in #{R_{\mathrm{elim}}}. On #{R_{\mathrm{elim}}^2},
define #{q_{\mathrm{eff}}(y)=y^TSy}; its half-polar matrix is
#{S}. Thus both #{q} and #{q_{\mathrm{eff}}} are
nondegenerate on this open. On the positive real locus,
#{q_{\mathrm{eff}}/2} is the quadratic part of the effective
action; the Gaussian normalization constant is additional.}

\p{The domain #{R_q} embeds in its fraction field, so these
entries can be tested in each local ring. If a prime
#{\mathfrak p} contains #{s}, the off-diagonal numerator
#{-2t} is congruent to #{4} modulo #{\mathfrak p} and is
a unit in #{(R_q)_{\mathfrak p}}. If #{(-2t)/s} were
regular there, multiplication by #{s} would equate an element
of the maximal ideal with a unit. This is impossible.
On #{D_{X_q}(s)} every entry is regular. Hence this is the
exact regular locus of the displayed Schur matrix.}

\p{For #{L=Q-I}, the diagonalization in
\ref{fdip-000U} gives eigenvalues #{0,2,2t,2t+2}. Therefore
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}L&=4(t+1),\\
\operatorname{tr}(L^2)&=8(t^2+t+1),\\
R(t)&=\frac{\operatorname{tr}(L^2)}{(\operatorname{tr}L)^2}
=\frac{t^2+t+1}{2(t+1)^2}.
\end{aligned}}
The numerator is #{f^2-f+1}. At every prime containing #{f},
it has residue #{1}, so is a unit in the local ring.
Regularity of #{R} there would make
##{2f^2R=f^2-f+1}
an equality of an element of the maximal ideal and a unit,
again impossible. Where #{f} is invertible the formula is
regular. Thus the exact ratio locus is #{U_R}, and its
intersection with #{X_{\mathrm{elim}}} is
#{X_{\mathrm{obs}}}. The interpretation of these localizations
as regular functions is
\citet{Lemma 26.5.4, tag 01HR}{stacks2017stacks}.}
}

\p{The half-turn restricts to
#{J=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)}
on both coordinate blocks. The equal diagonal and equal
off-diagonal entries of #{S} give #{J^TSJ=S}. Retained
projection #{\pi} satisfies #{\pi P=J\pi}, but is not an
isometry: #{q(\delta_1)=s} is a unit on
#{R_{\mathrm{elim}}}, whereas #{\pi\delta_1=0}.
Thus [Clifford functoriality](fdip-000Q) supplies no map induced
by this projection. The effective rank-two Clifford algebra
and the full rank-four algebra are different constructions.}

\p{Three rational specializations separate the domains:}
\ul{
\li{At #{t=-2}, #{\det Q=9} and #{R=3/2}, but
#{C_{\mathrm{block}}=0}. This point belongs to #{U_R}
inside #{X_q} and lies outside #{X_{\mathrm{elim}}}.}
\li{At #{t=-1}, #{\det Q=-3},
#{C_{\mathrm{block}}=I_2}, and
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\begin{pmatrix}-1&2\\2&-1\end{pmatrix},\\
\det S&=-3.
\end{aligned}}
This point belongs to #{X_{\mathrm{elim}}}, but the trace
ratio has a pole, so it lies outside #{X_{\mathrm{obs}}}.}
\li{At #{t=1}, #{\det Q=45},
#{C_{\mathrm{block}}=3I_2}, and
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\frac13\begin{pmatrix}7&-2\\-2&7\end{pmatrix},\\
\det S&=5,\qquad R=\frac38.
\end{aligned}}
This point belongs to #{X_{\mathrm{obs}}}.}
}

\p{For real #{t=\tau}, the matrix #{H/2} is orthogonal,
and the four eigenvalues of #{Q(\tau)} are
#{1,3,2\tau+1,2\tau+3}. They are all positive exactly
when #{\tau>-1/2}. The running weighted graph assumes the
smaller domain #{\tau>0}. Each such evaluation sends
#{2,\Delta,s,f} to nonzero reals and extends uniquely to
##{R_{\mathrm{obs}}\longrightarrow\mathbb R,\qquad t\longmapsto\tau.}
It gives #{\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb R\to X_{\mathrm{obs}}}
and recovers #{Q(\tau),S(\tau),P,J,R(\tau)}. The
[Clifford base-change isomorphism](fdip-000V) transports their
full-form algebra and symmetry; it does not require evaluation
to be injective. Gaussian integration and the
[normalized marginal](fdip-000E) apply on this positive real
locus. Algebraic invertibility alone gives neither positivity
nor a probability measure over the arithmetic base.}

\p{This family retains a parameter, residue characteristics,
and distinct regularity domains. It does not select a unique
arithmetic realization. For example, at #{t=1} modulo #{11},
#{\Delta=45}, #{s=3}, and #{f=2} are all nonzero. The point
therefore lies in #{X_{\mathrm{obs}}}, but disappears after
further inverting #{11}. Every real specialization survives
that extra inversion. Thus the same real family admits
different arithmetic opens. The construction predicts neither
a real parameter nor a physical observable from arithmetic
alone; an L-value comparison needs additional structure.}
